#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""
yt-framecraft — turn any YouTube video into a frame-perfect MP4 for AI vision.

SPEED-FIRST PIPELINE (v1.2):
  * Mirrors are raced first — yt-dlp is only the fallback, so a bot-walled
    YouTube costs zero seconds.
  * The source stream is capped at 720p by default (--hq for full res):
    ~3x smaller download, ~2x faster encode — plenty for AI vision.
  * The video downloads over multiple parallel HTTP range connections
    (--connections, default 4) instead of one slow stream.
  * Audio downloads concurrently with the video and is raced across the
    fastest mirrors so one slow proxy can't stall the pipeline (--no-audio
    skips it entirely for pure vision use).
  * Encode defaults to x264 superfast CRF 23 with the audio stream copied
    straight through (no audio re-encode) — ~2.5x realtime on two cores.
  * Every frame still lands exactly on the clock: constant frame rate,
    deinterlaced, yuv420p H.264, closed GOPs with keyframes every second,
    faststart, burned-in frame numbers + timestamps. A JSON sidecar describes
    the result so AI pipelines can reference frames exactly
    ("frame 1234 @ 41.133s, 30.000 fps").

Usage:
  yt-framecraft https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dQw4w9WgXcQ
  yt-framecraft dQw4w9WgXcQ --hq            # full-res source, crf 18
  yt-framecraft dQw4w9WgXcQ --fps 24 --scale 1280
  yt-framecraft --file lecture.mp4 -o lecture_ai.mp4
  yt-framecraft --file clip.mp4 --frames png_out --grid sheets
  yt-framecraft VIDEO_ID --mirror https://yt.chocolatemoo53.com   # force one mirror

Requires: ffmpeg on PATH (yt-dlp optional — mirrors are the fallback).
Python 3.8+, stdlib only.

Only download videos you have the rights to download.
"""

